            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a thank-you letter to someone who shared plant remedies (e.g., "Thank you for teaching us about guanabana (soursop)."). Can respond to a text message about plant uses in a familiar way (e.g., "Sara will remember to use sabila (aloe vera) for burns."). Can reply with simple feedback to a message about medicinal plants (e.g., "Thank you for the information on sabila (aloe vera).").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of soursop (guanabana) and aloe vera (sábila). Students are asked to write down one word they associate with each plant (e.g., "fruit," "health," "gel," "burns"). This activates prior vocabulary and prepares them for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two simple sentences on the board: "Guanabana is used to treat infections." and "You should use aloe vera for burns." The teacher points out the 'used to' structure and 'should' for giving advice. The teacher models the pronunciation of compound nouns like "medicinal plant" and "healing herb," stressing the first syllable, and clarifies correct spelling for "treatment" and "therapeutic."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to say *why* we use a plant, which words do I use: 'used to treat' or 'is treating'?" (Expected answer: "used to treat").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to give someone advice about using a plant, do I say 'You should' or 'You are'?" (Expected answer: "You should").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, handwritten thank-you note from a student to an elder for sharing information about medicinal plants (e.g., guanabana). The note includes sentences using the present simple, infinitive of purpose, and 'should'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Sample Text:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Dear Ms. Elena,</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Thank you for teaching us about guanabana (soursop). It is a very useful plant. The leaves are used to soothe pain. You should also drink the tea for infections.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Sincerely,</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Student's Name]"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will recognize the purpose and structure of a simple thank-you letter/message related to sharing health advice about plants.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Written):</b> Students read the letter and answer two written questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main purpose of this letter?" (e.g., to thank someone)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which plant is mentioned in the letter, and what is it used for?" (e.g., guanabana, used to soothe pain and for infections).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using the infinitive of purpose ("used to + verb"), `should/shouldn't` for advice, and the present simple for facts, incorporating target vocabulary (e.g., plant, leaf, root, remedy, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, treat, heal, soothe, apply).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings and students complete them, focusing on the target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "Mint leaves are ______ (use) ______ headaches." (Expected: "used to relieve"). "For a burn, you _______ (use) aloe vera." (Expected: "should use"). Students write these sentences in their notebooks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-Controlled Practice - Guided Sentence Writing):</b> Students are given a list of plants (e.g., ginger, lemongrass) and associated uses/advice words (e.g., "boil," "colds," "infections," "root," "tea"). In pairs, students write two complete sentences for each plant, one stating its purpose and one giving advice. Example: "Ginger root is used to treat infections. You should boil the root to make a tea." (Teacher circulates, checking written work and providing feedback on spelling and grammar.) Students are reminded to apply syllable division rules for words like "treatment" and "therapeutic" for correct spelling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Check & Feedback - Written):</b> Students exchange their written sentences from Activity 2 with a partner. They check each other's sentences for correct use of `used to`, `should/shouldn't`, present simple, and the target vocabulary. They also check for spelling and clarity of meaning. Partners provide written feedback (e.g., "Good use of 'should'!" or "Check spelling of 'remedy'.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual writing task: Students will compose a simple thank-you letter or email reply.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Imagine you received a message (email/text) from a friend who shared information about a traditional plant remedy. Write a short thank-you email or letter in reply. In your reply, thank your friend for the information, mention one plant remedy they shared (e.g., guanabana or aloe vera), state its purpose, and give one piece of advice using 'should/shouldn't'. Use at least four of the target vocabulary words (e.g., plant, remedy, treat, soothe, apply, leaf, soursop, aloe vera, useful, medicinal, natural).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example sentence starters provided as support:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Hi [Friend's Name],"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Thank you for..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"[Plant Name] is a useful plant."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is used to..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should/shouldn't..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Best,"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to write neatly and focus on clear communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the writing task, using a simple checklist to note student's ability to: 1) use `used to` for purpose, 2) use `should/shouldn't` for advice, 3) incorporate target vocabulary, and 4) convey a clear thank-you message.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their thank-you letter/email reply to the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Legibility:</b> The message is easy to read and understand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses at least 3-4 words from the lesson's vocabulary list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correctly uses "used to + infinitive" and "should/shouldn't" in sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Successfully thanks the friend, mentions a plant remedy, states its purpose, and gives advice.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Written):</b> Students answer the following prompts in writing before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned today about writing about plants is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to (circle one) write advice using 'should'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the thank-you letter was the most challenging for you, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can writing about healing plants be useful in your daily life or for sharing information with others?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The practice activities allowed students to build confidence with the grammatical structures before the production task. The modeling of a simple thank-you note was effective. Some students still struggled with spelling longer vocabulary words like "therapeutic," requiring more explicit phonemic awareness practice in future lessons. Next time, I will provide more varied sentence starters for the advice section. The overall engagement with writing about local plants was high. Students generally achieved the objective of composing a simple thank-you letter with support, and could reply to a message in written form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing practice will prepare students for the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), where they will consolidate and summarize information about various healing plants. They will use their accumulated vocabulary and grammatical structures to present or explain information about remedies, drawing on their ability to structure written responses and advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>